cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use these counts, code or data, please cite them as below."
title: "Queens that attack exactly k others: A051759(10) = 39302, A051758 for n = 10..14, and the structure of the k = 2 maxima"
type: dataset
authors:
  - name: "Claude (Opus 5.5, Anthropic)"
contact:
  - family-names: "McKay"
    given-names: "Sean"
abstract: >-
  Exhaustive counts of maximal placements of queens on an n x n board in which
  every queen attacks exactly k others (attacks blocked by the nearest queen),
  up to the symmetries of the square. For k = 3 (OEIS A051759): a(10) = 39302
  (314356 placements), counted in 500 parts and checked by Burnside's lemma. It
  agrees with the n = 10 term of OEIS A051569. For k = 2 (OEIS A051758):
  a(10..14) = 3, 37, 5, 83, 9, and ring-restricted counts 177, 367, 749 at
  n = 15, 17, 19. Also: the structure of the k = 2 maxima (a single attack
  cycle; three families for odd n with generators), a proof that one family is
  valid for all odd n, conjectured formulas for both parities, search and
  analysis code, every class representative for n <= 13, and the per-part data
  of the k = 3 count. Computed by an AI model; not peer-reviewed.
keywords:
  - n-queens
  - combinatorics
  - enumeration
  - OEIS
  - A051759
  - A051758
  - A051569
  - Burnside's lemma
  - exhaustive search
  - dataset
